.Dd September 29, 2026
.Dt BOSD-START 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm bosd-start
.Nd start the default bosd daemon
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Xr bosd 1
paints a glyph, a gauge, or a caption with or without a daemon.
With no daemon listening, it paints the request in its own window
and exits when the hold elapses.
That is a complete show.
.Pp
A daemon,
.Nm bosd
with
.Fl d ,
keeps one overlay for the instance and repaints it in place.
A gauge, a glyph, and a small caption each run on their own timer,
so they can share the screen, and a later request replaces its own
slot.
The client sends the request and returns.
Key repeat updates the window already up.
.Pp
.Nm
starts that daemon for the default instance.
.Nm bosd
with
.Fl d
stays in the foreground.
A startup script that wants it has to background it, and the shell
returns when the process has been forked, before the request socket
exists.
.Nm
backgrounds
.Nm bosd
and returns when the socket is there, so the first client of the
session finds a daemon.
.Pp
Call
.Nm
from each place a session can start: a window manager
.Ic StartFunction ,
an XDG autostart entry, and the login greeter.
Those paths are separate, and any of them may run first.
When the default daemon is already running, and its executable is
the one
.Nm
resolved on
.Ev PATH ,
the call returns.
.Pp
The same job dropped into a window manager rc looks like
.Bd -literal -offset indent
bosd -d &
.Ed
.Pp
That line runs when that window manager starts.
It returns before the socket exists, so an early client paints on
its own and exits.
A second start contends for the socket the first daemon holds.
.Nm
is safe to run from each startup path, and it returns after the
socket is ready.
.Pp
When the recorded pid is running some other binary, that process is
stopped and a new daemon is started.
Installing a new
.Nm bosd
leaves the process mapped from the old file.
The next
.Nm
is what puts the new binary on the socket.
.Pp
A named channel
.Po
.Nm bosd
.Fl n Ar instance
.Pc
is started by whoever uses that channel.
.Nm
starts the default instance.
.Pp
When
.Ev BOSD_PATH
is unset and
.Pa ~/theme/osd
exists, the daemon is given that directory.
Otherwise
.Nm bosd
uses the icon directory it was built with.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width "BOSD_PATH"
.It Ev BOSD_PATH
Colon-separated icon search path handed to the daemon.
When unset,
.Pa ~/theme/osd
is used if that directory exists.
.It Ev DISPLAY
X11 display.
The default is
.Pa :0 .
.It Ev HOME
Used to find
.Pa ~/theme/osd
when
.Ev BOSD_PATH
is unset.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "/tmp/bosd.default.uid.sock" -compact
.It Pa /tmp/bosd.default. Ns Ar uid Ns Pa .sock
request socket of the default instance
.It Pa /tmp/bosd.default. Ns Ar uid Ns Pa .pid
daemon pid
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr bosd 1
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Devin Teske Aq Mt dteske@FreeBSD.org
